(*Part R. The revised hypotheses: the superfluid as the Higgs field of the Standard Model*)

(* ::Text:: *)
(*On 21 November 2026, at 17:09 PST, the author asked to modify the hypotheses so that the superfluid can be the Higgs field of the Standard Model, with the same quantum numbers as the Higgs doublet; at 19:38 PST he added a second singlet field of hypercharge $-\tfrac34$. Parts I to III of this notebook state the earlier hypotheses, under which the spinor field carries no electroweak quantum numbers. This part states the revised hypotheses and repeats, in the Wolfram Language and in exact arithmetic wherever that is possible, the checks of the four stages of the revision that were computed by Rust programs: the quantum numbers of the fields and of their pairs (Section R.1, program `sh_quantum`), the ground state of the superfluid (R.2, `sh_superfluid`), the masses of the gauge bosons and the modes of the superfluid (R.3, `sh_higgs`) and the dynamics in time (R.4, `sh_dynamics`). The five remarks made during the revision, R8 to R12, are stated where they belong, each with the computation on which it rests, what it means and why it matters.*)

(* ::Section:: *)
(*R.0 The fields, the interaction, and how the checks of this part are named*)

(* ::Text:: *)
(***The fields.** In the second version of the revised hypotheses, the current one, there are three Dirac fields, all singlets of colour and all of the mass $m_{\rm LL}=0.2$ in units of the momentum cutoff $\Lambda_{\rm LL}=1$: the isodoublet $\Psi_{\rm LL}$, an up and a down component, each a Dirac spinor of four components, with the hypercharge $Y^{\rm SM}=\tfrac14$; the singlet $\chi_{\rm SH}$, with the hypercharge $\tfrac14$; and the singlet $\chi'_{\rm SH}$, with the hypercharge $-\tfrac34$. The electric charges $Q^{\rm SM}=T^{3,{\rm SM}}+Y^{\rm SM}$, with $T^{a,{\rm SM}}$ ($a=1,2,3$) the generators of $SU(2)_L$, are $\tfrac34$ (up), $-\tfrac14$ (down), $\tfrac14$ ($\chi_{\rm SH}$) and $-\tfrac34$ ($\chi'_{\rm SH}$); antiparticles carry the opposite charges. The first version of the revised hypotheses, which this part keeps as the comparison, had no $\chi'_{\rm SH}$. The code numbers the sixteen components of the field $(\Psi_{\rm LL},\chi_{\rm SH},\chi'_{\rm SH})$ from 0: the components $0,\dots,7$ belong to $\Psi_{\rm LL}$ (the component $2d+s$ has the Dirac index $d=0,\dots,3$ and the isospin index $s$, 0 for up and 1 for down), $8,\dots,11$ to $\chi_{\rm SH}$ and $12,\dots,15$ to $\chi'_{\rm SH}$. The Dirac matrices are those of the chiral basis, $\gamma^0_{\rm LL}=\begin{pmatrix}0&\mathsf 1_2\\\mathsf 1_2&0\end{pmatrix}$, with the chirality $\gamma^{\rm ch}_{\rm LL}=i\gamma^0_{\rm LL}\gamma^1_{\rm LL}\gamma^2_{\rm LL}\gamma^3_{\rm LL}={\rm diag}(-1,-1,1,1)$ and the charge-conjugation matrix $C_{\rm LL}=i\gamma^2_{\rm LL}\gamma^0_{\rm LL}$.*)

(* ::Text:: *)
(***The symmetries.** Besides the gauge group $SU(2)_L\times U(1)_Y$, the free fields have the global groups generated by the particle number $F^{\rm tot}_{\rm SH}$ (one for every particle of the three fields), by the relative number $R_{\rm SH}$ (the number of particles of $\Psi_{\rm LL}$ minus that of the two singlets) and by the generators $T^{a,{\rm R}}_{\rm SH}$ of the custodial group $SU(2)_{\rm R}$, which rotates the pair $(\chi_{\rm SH},\chi'_{\rm SH})$ as a doublet. The hypercharge is the combination*)

(* ::DisplayFormula:: *)
(*Y^{\rm SM}=T^{3,{\rm R}}_{\rm SH}+\tfrac14R_{\rm SH},\qquad Q^{\rm SM}=T^{3,{\rm SM}}+T^{3,{\rm R}}_{\rm SH}+\tfrac14R_{\rm SH}.*)

(* ::Text:: *)
(***The interaction.** The Hamiltonian density contains the free Dirac terms of the three fields, the scalar and the vector contact terms of $\Psi_{\rm LL}$ of Parts II and III, with the couplings $G^{\rm S}_{\rm SF}=-8$ and $G^{\rm V}_{\rm SF}=0.8$, and the pairing terms*)

(* ::DisplayFormula:: *)
(*-G_{\rm SH}\sum_{s={\rm u},{\rm d}}\Big[{:}(\Pi^s_{\rm SH})^\dagger\Pi^s_{\rm SH}{:}+{:}(\Pi'^s_{\rm SH})^\dagger\Pi'^s_{\rm SH}{:}\Big],\qquad\Pi^s_{\rm SH}=\tfrac12\Psi^T_{\rm LL}\big(C_{\rm LL}\gamma^{\rm ch}_{\rm LL}\otimes\mathbf e^s_{\rm SH}\big)\chi_{\rm SH},\qquad\Pi'^s_{\rm SH}=\tfrac12\Psi^T_{\rm LL}\big(C_{\rm LL}\gamma^{\rm ch}_{\rm LL}\otimes\mathbf e^s_{\rm SH}\big)\chi'_{\rm SH},*)

(* ::Text:: *)
(*with $G_{\rm SH}=100.8$, the isospin columns $\mathbf e^{\rm u}_{\rm SH}=(1,0)^T$ and $\mathbf e^{\rm d}_{\rm SH}=(0,1)^T$, and ${:}\ {:}$ the normal ordering with respect to the normal vacuum. $\Pi^s_{\rm SH}$ annihilates a particle of the component $s$ of $\Psi_{\rm LL}$ together with a particle of $\chi_{\rm SH}$ in the state of spin zero and positive parity, and $\Pi'^s_{\rm SH}$ does the same with a particle of $\chi'_{\rm SH}$. The expectation values $\Phi_{\rm SH}=(\Phi^{\rm u}_{\rm SH},\Phi^{\rm d}_{\rm SH})$ of $(\Pi^{\rm u}_{\rm SH},\Pi^{\rm d}_{\rm SH})$ and $\Phi'_{\rm SH}$ of $(\Pi'^{\rm u}_{\rm SH},\Pi'^{\rm d}_{\rm SH})$ are the two pair fields, and the superfluid is their condensate. The ground state is computed by the five mean-field methods of Part II: HS (1, 1), HS (0, 1) and HS (0, 0) (the method of Hubbard and Stratonovich with three parameter sets), KS (Kohn and Sham) and CJT (Cornwall, Jackiw and Tomboulis).*)

(* ::Text:: *)
(***How the checks of this part are named.** Each check of this part is one of the checks of the four Wolfram scripts of the revision, `wolfram/source/sh_quantum.wls`, `sh_superfluid.wls`, `sh_higgs.wls` and `sh_dynamics.wls`, under the same identifier: W, the letters of the stage (QN, SF, H, D) and a number. The scripts were run on the author's computer, and their reports are kept with the data of the Rust programs; this part repeats their code with the explanations a reader needs. The checks of the three programs of a stage do not correspond one to one: one exact check of the Wolfram Language often derives what several Rust checks test in floating point, and a few checks have no counterpart. Every check therefore names, after its result, the checks of the Rust program and of the Python program that test the same statements, or "none". In stage 2 the identifiers follow the families of the Rust checks (WQN-4 tests statements of QN-4a to QN-4c); in the other stages they are numbered in the order of the scripts (decision D34 of the task record).*)

